# Copy to remote/hosts.env (gitignored) and fill in the servers the remote
# chain sets up. Any value can also be passed as an environment variable of
# the same name, which takes precedence over this file; REMOTE_HOSTS_FILE
# points the chain at a different file.
#
# Servers: fresh Ubuntu 24.04 installs (any provider), reachable over ssh
# as SSH_USER, who needs passwordless sudo (the default for cloud images'
# ubuntu user, or use root). At least:
#   control plane: 2 vCPUs, 4 GB RAM, 30 GB disk - Dokploy, its database,
#     Traefik, the registry, OpenBao and the apps' databases all run here
#   workers: 2 vCPUs, 2 GB RAM, 15 GB disk - each runs a CI runner, whose
#     jobs need about 9 GB of disk at peak
#
# Firewall: every node gets a port guard (remote/node/dokploy-port-guard)
# that closes Dokploy (3000), the registry (5000) and OpenBao (8200) to the
# network, and Docker Swarm's ports (2377/tcp, 7946/tcp+udp, 4789/udp) to
# everything but the cluster's own nodes. The chain reaches Dokploy, the
# registry and OpenBao through ssh. So the provider's firewall only needs:
#   22/tcp to every node, from this machine (ideally from nowhere else)
#   80/tcp and 443/tcp to the control plane, from anywhere: the apps
#   2377/tcp, 7946/tcp+udp and 4789/udp between the nodes
# To open Dokploy's UI, tunnel to it: the scripts print the ssh command.

# Public (or otherwise reachable-from-here) IPv4 address of the control plane.
CP_HOST="203.0.113.10"
# Space-separated IPv4 addresses of the workers, in order; may be empty.
WORKER_HOSTS="203.0.113.11 203.0.113.12"

# Address the workers reach the control plane on, and so the network the
# swarm's traffic crosses. Defaults to the control plane's own primary
# address: its public IP, or its private one on clouds that NAT the public
# IP onto a private network (AWS, GCP). Set it to the control plane's
# address on a private network to keep the swarm's traffic on that network
# (it isn't encrypted) - but only if that network's MTU is 1500: Swarm's
# overlay networks fail on smaller ones (some providers' private networks,
# e.g. Hetzner's, use 1450). The chain checks and refuses if it isn't.
# CP_SWARM_ADDR="10.0.0.10"

SSH_USER="ubuntu"
# SSH_PORT="22"
# Defaults to your ssh agent / ~/.ssh/config.
# SSH_KEY="~/.ssh/id_ed25519"
